outputs.with:
**Finding (Correctness — Blocking):** Using `parseInt(price)` drops decimal digits from currency strings (turning `"9.95"` into `9`) and implicitly ignores trailing non-numeric characters. Parse the trimmed string with `Number` and reject empty or non-finite values at the boundary.

**Scope & Checks:** Static code review of the single line only; no tests or typecheck were run.

**Correction:**
```ts
const normalized = price.trim();
const amount = Number(normalized);
if (normalized === "" || !Number.isFinite(amount)) {
  throw new Error(`Expected a numeric currency string, received: ${price}`);
}
```

**Regression Test:**
```ts
import assert from "node:assert/strict";

const parseAmount = (price: string): number => {
  const normalized = price.trim();
  const amount = Number(normalized);
  if (normalized === "" || !Number.isFinite(amount)) {
    throw new Error(`Expected a numeric currency string, received: ${price}`);
  }
  return amount;
};

assert.equal(parseAmount("9.95"), 9.95);
assert.equal(parseAmount(" 19.90 "), 19.9);
assert.throws(() => parseAmount(""));
assert.throws(() => parseAmount("9.95USD"));
```
